[Music]  how do you dream about changing the  world  maybe it's by starting a company that  helps women look and feel their best  creating hundreds of jobs and  internships  maybe you're successful in manufacturing  first before you turn to philanthropy  changing children's lives forever  or maybe it's by getting elected to the  west wing tonight I'd like to suggest a  different but just as a rewarding option  serving in your local government  sometimes it feels hard to separate  government from a TV comedy and when you  can't take government seriously it's  hard to care about it or value it early  feedback for this talk included could  you find a different word for government  it turns people off when you say it so  much I haven't found any good synonyms  for government yet but tonight I want to  make the case that you should take it  seriously  and consider getting involved with your  City Hall or State Capitol because state  and local government is the most  impactful organization that you've never  cared about and all of you smart  talented people should care about an  organization that moves billions of  dollars and affects millions of lives in  our community right now participation in  local government is low across the board  only 19 percent of Americans have  contacted a local official in the last  year only 24 percent have attended a  local public meeting those rates are  even lower amongst young Americans but  when I asked my friends here if they'd  consider getting involved I hear things  like it's not  the highest and best use of their time  or that government is slow and  inefficient there's this perception that  your frustrating experience at the DMV  would translate into the impact that you  could have by getting involved and I  can't promise you that government offers  the autonomy that you might have at a  startup or will be as fast-moving as a  tech company but I also don't think any  of us would like it if our local police  force or fire department started using  move fast and break things as their  motto  slowness in government is a design  feature it prevents any one of us from  consolidating power and allows all of us  to have a say in how we are governed and  I can promise you that the slowness that  you sometimes see in government does not  translate into the impact that you could  have by getting involved there are three  reasons why influence scale and talent  let's talk about influence take Cameron  Johnson who graduated from the GSB in  2008 these days Cameron is the director  of product innovation at Netflix  he's also the vice mayor of San Carlos  California during the day Cameron works  on video streaming when you turn your  field phone from portrait to landscape  mode at night he goes to transportation  board meetings where he decides on  policies for roads public transit and  autonomous vehicles for the 7 million  people who live in the South Bay Cameron  works on your user experience on the  Netflix app and on the 101 I've seen  this ability to have immediate influence  firsthand when I was 25 years old I  worked with the mayor of Salt Lake  County on a project to help the homeless  and men leaving the county jail at 25 I  got to chair committees coordinate data  sharing agreements and launched an 11  million dollar project that provided job  training rehabilitation services and  housing for over 300 men so what's the  multiplier effect of that influence  let's talk about scale you could for  example go work at the Gates Foundation  which just gave 1.7 billion dollars to  education that's scale right the man  himself Bill Gates actually said it best  in his Foundation's annual letter while  our foundation is the largest in the  world the amount we give is small  compared to government the Gates  Foundation  endowment a forty four billion dollars  is less than the seventy six billion  dollars that the state of California  spends on its public school system in  one year and it's not just education if  you care about health care energy  transportation chances are that the  government deploys far more resources  than philanthropy does lastly I want to  talk about talent there's a term in  baseball called value over replacement  player it argues that a batter's value  isn't the absolute number of runs he  hits but the runs he hits over the  average player who would have replaced  him think about your own value over a  replacement for example if you're going  into consulting you'll be joining the  nine hundred and eighty five other GSB  graduates since 2003 so what happens  when you leave Bane or BCG in a few  years they'll likely replace you with  someone ninety five percent is good from  HBS the same is not true of government  since 2003 only twenty two of us or half  of 1% have gone directly into government  out of over thirty three thousand alums  in the lam Knight directory only eighty  eight are involved in state and local  government that means that our cities  and states just don't have that many  people who think about traffic and  congestion with an Operations  perspective or run a local public health  campaign with a marketing background or  even higher key personnel with  recommendations from Joel Peterson your  value over a placement could be high as  you leave the GSB you're looking for  ways to have impact quickly it's why  people do search funds to run an  organization a few years after  graduating it's why people do consulting  to influence large powerful  organizations  it's why people do private equity to  deploy large pools of capital to improve  organizations you can do all three of  these things a mile down the road at  City Hall I've spent this whole talk  talking about what you could do for a  government but in the end I'd ask that  you value government for what it does  for you  local government takes out your trash it  repairs the roads that you drive on and  pays the policeman who pulls you over  for speeding it likely runs the schools  that you attended as a child and the  schools that your children will attend  it decides whether you have a composting  bin or whether to utilize renewable  energy for millions of residents it runs  hospitals provides health care and cares  for the elderly these essential services  aren't just things that happen to exist  in our communities they were developed  and they can be improved by you and by  me Teddy Roosevelt once said far and  away the best prize that life has to  offer is the chance to work hard at work  worth doing you all have a lot of  options in how you change the world but  I hope that you consider public service  it is hard work but it is work worth  doing  [Music]

